UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 31, Lesson 2: Rehearsing scenes', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Rehearsing scripted scenes moves from on-book to off-book. Partners may prompt with a single word if agreed. Write notes after each run: clarity of objective, volume, eye line. Keep scripts off the floor to avoid trips.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nDesign a three-run rehearsal plan to get off-book safely.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.''\n\nWATCH FOR\nSnatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRehearse a scene through three runs with a notes column completed after each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Rehearsing scripted scenes moves from on-book to off-book. Partners may prompt with a single word if agreed. Write notes after each run: clarity of objective, volume, eye line. Keep scripts off the floor to avoid trips.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Rehearse a scene through three runs with a notes column completed after each. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.'' Reasoning: I used rehearse scene by attending to off-book rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages. Correction: Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.'' Why: the correction restores accurate use of rehearse scene.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating rehearse scene on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with rehearse scene, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.'' This matches the lesson skill for rehearse scene.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.''","Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages."],"question":"Design a three-run rehearsal plan to get off-book safely. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages.","Rehearsing scripted scenes moves from on-book to off-book. Partners may prompt with a single word if agreed. Write notes after each run: clarity of objective,…","Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.''","Careful use of off-book with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising rehearse scene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named off-book, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the off-book used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"A learner is practising rehearse scene. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.'' uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.''"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about rehearse scene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about rehearse scene.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about off-book?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: Run 1 on-book, Run 2 half off-book with prompts, Run 3 off-book. Note: ''Need stronger reaction on line 12.''","Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Rehearsing scripted scenes moves from on-book to off-book. Partners may prompt with a single word if agreed.… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages.","Rehearse a scene through three runs with a notes column completed after each.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of rehearse scene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating rehearse scene on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.'' Reasoning: I used rehearse scene by attending to off-book rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with rehearse scene, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages. Correction: Strong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.'' Why: the correction restores accurate use of rehearse scene.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w31-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise rehearse scene.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use rehearse scene with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify off-book and explain why it matters.","I can practise prompt independently on a new prompt.","I can notice notes needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Rehearsing scripted scenes moves from on-book to off-book. Partners may prompt with a single word if agreed. Write notes after each run: clarity of objective, volume, eye line. Keep scripts off the floor to avoid trips. Supported responses name the element or technique and its…","options":["Rehearsing scripted scenes moves from on-book to off-book. Partners may prompt with a single word if agreed. Write notes after each run: clarity of objective, volume, eye line. Keep scripts off the floor to avoid trips.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about rehearse scene?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Run 1 on-book, Run 2 half off-book with prompts, Run 3 off-book. Note: ''Need stronger reaction on line 12.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDesign a three-run rehearsal plan to get off-book safely.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''On-book accuracy; prompt-allowed; off-book with notes—no shaming for prompts.''","explain":"Rehearsing scripted scenes moves from on-book to off-book. Partners may prompt with a single word if agreed. Write notes after each run: clarity of objective, volume, eye line. Keep scripts off the floor to avoid trips.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Snatching scripts away to force memory. Confidence builds in stages. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Rehearsal stages.","caption":"Build confidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">On-book</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">accuracy first</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Off-book</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">memory later</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Run 1 on-book, Run 2 half off-book with prompts, Run 3 off-book. Note: ''Need stronger reaction on line 12.'' Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Run 1 on-book, Run 2 half off-book with prompts, Run 3 off-book. Note: ''Need stronger reaction on line 12.'' Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about rehearse scene?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Run 1 on-book, Run 2 half off-book with prompts, Run 3 off-book. Note: ''Need stronger reaction on line 12.'' Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Prompts and notes.","caption":"Kind, specific.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\